<edge source="north_korea_standoff::escalation_risk" target="russia_ukraine_war::external_aid_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.65</data>
  <data key="d36">A spike in North Korea escalation risk prompts the United States and partners to withhold or redirect shipments of air‑defense munitions and precision munitions to Eastern Europe so stocks and transport are retained for immediate deterrence/reassurance in Northeast Asia.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">north_korea_standoff</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="north_korea_standoff::escalation_risk" target="tech_semiconductor_rival::trade_controls_ai" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">A spike in standoff escalation provokes rapid government decisions to impose or tighten export restrictions and sanctions on AI-relevant chips and components to deny an adversary battlefield-relevant capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">north_korea_standoff</data>
  <data key="d39">tech_semiconductor_rival</data>
</edge>
<edge source="north_korea_standoff::escalation_risk" target="tech_semiconductor_rival::supply_chain_lead_times" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Heightened military risk raises shipping interruptions, port congestion, and insurance and inspection burdens, forcing suppliers to reroute shipments and increasing end-to-end lead times for AI hardware components.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">north_korea_standoff</data>
  <data key="d39">tech_semiconductor_rival</data>
</edge>
<edge source="north_korea_standoff::escalation_risk" target="us_treasury_market_stres::secondary_market_liquidi" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">A sudden rise in standoff escalation triggers a flight-to-quality and order imbalances that concentrate trading in on-the-run Treasuries, overwhelming dealer inventories and market‑making capacity and widening bid/ask spreads, thereby reducing secondary‑market liquidity.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">north_korea_standoff</data>
  <data key="d39">us_treasury_market_stres</data>
</edge>
<edge source="north_korea_standoff::escalation_risk" target="us_treasury_market_stres::short_term_funding_liqui" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">Escalation increases counterparty risk perceptions and margin volatility, causing banks and repo counterparties to hoard cash and tighten collateral acceptance, which directly reduces short‑term funding availability and raises funding costs for Treasury market participants.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">north_korea_standoff</data>
  <data key="d39">us_treasury_market_stres</data>
</edge>
<edge source="north_korea_standoff::escalation_risk" target="us_treasury_market_stres::investor_leverage_positi" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A sharp uptick in standoff risk leads leveraged bond funds and hedge funds to face margin calls and reduce gross leverage and directional positions to de‑risk portfolios, lowering aggregate investor leverage and duration concentration in affected markets.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">north_korea_standoff</data>
  <data key="d39">us_treasury_market_stres</data>
</edge>
